  <name><text>Ch09_P03_More_failures</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>(a) The first conjecture fails at \(n = 41\) and \(n = 42\), where \(n^2 - n + 41\) is divisible by 41. Find a value of \(n\) greater than 42 for which \(n^2 - n + 41\) is divisible by 41.</p>
<div>\(n =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) \(n^2 - n + 41\) is divisible by 41 exactly when \(n\) leaves certain remainders on division by 41. Enter the set of these remainders, for example <code>{3, 7}</code>.</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Write \(n^2 - n + 41 = n(n - 1) + 41\). If \(n\) is a multiple of 41, or one more than a multiple of 41, then \(n(n - 1)\) is a multiple of 41, and so is \(n^2 - n + 41\). For example \(n = 82\) gives \(82 \times 81 + 41 = 41(2 \times 81 + 1) = 41 \times 163\), and \(n = 83\) gives \(41 \times 2 \times 83 + 41 = 41 \times 167\).</p>
<p>Conversely, since 41 is prime, 41 divides \(n(n - 1)\) only if it divides \(n\) or \(n - 1\). So the remainders are 0 and 1: the set \(\{0, 1\}\).</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
